# 4. EXACT USER ROLE MODEL

There are exactly three application roles.

## Role A: Participant

Every normal public registration creates a Participant account.

Participants can:

- Browse public events.
- Register for eligible events.
- Create or join teams.
- Invite teammates.
- Select problem statements.
- Submit authorized repository URLs.
- View their own submission status.
- View their own published evaluation marks.
- Read feedback released to them.
- View public leaderboards.
- Manage their own profile and notification preferences.

Participants cannot:

- Grant roles.
- Access admin dashboards.
- Read other teams' private evaluations.
- View unpublished scores.
- Access private code-similarity evidence.
- Change official scores or rankings.
- Publish results.

## Role B: Admin — Also the Judge

There is no separate Judge role. Admin is the judge role.

Admins can access events explicitly assigned to them and only the permissions granted for those events.

Core judging capabilities:

- View assigned teams.
- Review assigned problem statements.
- Inspect authorized repository snapshots.
- Review commit timelines and contributor activity.
- Inspect code-similarity findings and evidence.
- Enter criterion-level marks.
- Add comments and evaluation notes.
- Save drafts.
- Submit evaluations.
- View their permitted evaluation history.

Additional event-management permissions may be granted by the Superadmin:

- Manage team registrations.
- Manage problem assignments.
- Assign judging work within delegated limits.
- Monitor analysis jobs.
- Review evaluation completion.
- Correct or return evaluations.
- Prepare reports.
- Preview or publish results only when explicitly authorized.

An Admin must never automatically receive global Superadmin permissions.

## Role C: Superadmin

The Superadmin is the platform owner, controlled by me.

The Superadmin can:

- Manage users and account status.
- Grant and revoke Admin privileges.
- Create and manage all events.
- Assign Admins to events.
- Configure problem statements.
- Build evaluation schemas.
- Configure scoring and leaderboard rules.
- Review evaluations.
- Authorize mark overrides with mandatory audit reasons.
- Publish or correct results.
- Configure repository-analysis settings.
- Access global reports and audit logs.
- Manage platform settings.

## Privilege escalation protection

This is a critical requirement.

- Never expose an Admin or Superadmin role selector in public registration.
- Never trust a client-supplied role.
- Never allow users to write their own privileged role assignments.
- Protect role assignments using Firestore Security Rules and trusted Cloud Functions.
- Do not make the first Superadmin account dependent on a public signup flow.
- Provide a secure initial Superadmin provisioning procedure using a trusted administrative environment.
- Protect role revocation and suspension.
- Recheck permissions on sensitive backend operations.
- A user who loses privileges must not retain access through cached frontend state.
- Do not allow an Admin to grant themselves additional privileges.
- Keep event-specific permissions separate from platform-wide roles.

A user may have multiple explicitly authorized roles if needed, but event assignments and conflict-of-interest restrictions must still apply.

