# 23. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The application is not complete until the following are true:

### Roles

- Exactly three roles exist: Participant, Admin/Judge, Superadmin.
- New public users receive Participant access.
- Only authorized Superadmin operations can grant or revoke Admin privileges.
- Admins can access only assigned events and delegated operations.
- Participants cannot read private evaluation data.

### Events

- Superadmin can create and configure events.
- Admin assignments work.
- Participants can register and form teams.
- Problem selection follows configured rules.

### Repositories

- Participants can submit repositories.
- Repository snapshots identify exact commits.
- Analysis jobs have real status tracking.
- Repository evidence is linked to its source.
- Unsupported integrations are clearly reported rather than simulated.

### Evaluation

- Superadmin can create and publish dynamic schemas.
- Admins can evaluate assigned teams.
- Score bounds and required fields are validated.
- Historical schema versions remain preserved.
- Finalized evaluations cannot be silently overwritten.

### Scoring

- Trusted backend calculates official scores.
- Ranking follows configured rules.
- Public leaderboards contain only permitted fields.
- Results can be published and corrected with audit history.

### Security

- Firestore and Storage rules are restrictive.
- Sensitive operations validate authorization on the backend.
- Secrets are not exposed.
- Role escalation is blocked.
- Audit records exist for privileged changes.

### UX

- Every major route is implemented.
- Navigation works.
- Primary actions work.
- Forms validate.
- Loading, empty, success, and error states exist.
- Mobile layouts work without horizontal overflow.
- No core screen is merely a static mockup.

### Delivery

- The project builds successfully.
- TypeScript errors are resolved.
- Critical security and scoring logic has tests.
- README and setup instructions are complete.
- Missing external credentials or provider configuration are documented honestly.

